1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,563
In part 1, we built the index:
chunk, embed, load.

2
00:00:03,883 --> 00:00:05,483
Part 2 puts it to work.

3
00:00:05,803 --> 00:00:09,685
But first, a test:
does the index survive closing the laptop?

4
00:00:09,933 --> 00:00:11,746
Leaving isn't in the README.

5
00:00:12,066 --> 00:00:17,165
Type exit, and on the laptop,
list every container with docker ps -a.

6
00:00:17,485 --> 00:00:20,820
The CLI container is gone, removed on exit,

7
00:00:20,820 --> 00:00:24,931
but Chroma is still up,
and port 8000 is still taken.

8
00:00:25,167 --> 00:00:28,836
To stop it, run docker compose down
from the repo folder.

9
00:00:29,156 --> 00:00:31,610
Two warnings say the variables aren't set.

10
00:00:31,930 --> 00:00:36,452
They're harmless: only the script sets them,
and the down command needs neither.

11
00:00:36,772 --> 00:00:39,119
Then Chroma and the network are removed.

12
00:00:39,367 --> 00:00:40,860
Nothing is running now.

13
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:45,895
But the docker-volumes/ folder
on your disk still holds the database file

14
00:00:45,895 --> 00:00:47,402
and both index folders,

15
00:00:47,402 --> 00:00:50,481
and your chunk files
in outputs/ are untouched.

16
00:00:50,717 --> 00:00:54,429
Back another day: sh docker-shell.sh again.

17
00:00:54,749 --> 00:00:57,309
No pull, no build: the image exists.

18
00:00:57,629 --> 00:01:00,868
After a git pull
that changes the dependencies

19
00:01:00,868 --> 00:01:02,136
or the Dockerfile,

20
00:01:02,136 --> 00:01:04,285
run docker compose build first.

21
00:01:04,605 --> 00:01:05,927
Pause and restart.

22
00:01:09,367 --> 00:01:14,373
Two extra checks: both collections are back,
with dimension 256,

23
00:01:14,373 --> 00:01:19,458
and an extra command called --get
returns the same 10 record IDs.

24
00:01:19,778 --> 00:01:23,233
The container was disposable;
the data never lived in it.

25
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:25,991
Now the bottom lane.

26
00:01:26,311 --> 00:01:29,444
The question goes
through the same embedding model

27
00:01:29,444 --> 00:01:33,202
and becomes a query vector,
in the same space as the chunks.

28
00:01:33,522 --> 00:01:35,996
Querying means finding
its nearest neighbors.

29
00:01:36,233 --> 00:01:39,390
The lecture scores every stored vector
against the query.

30
00:01:39,710 --> 00:01:46,260
0.57, 0.77, 0.43, 0.93.

31
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:50,462
Sort by score, and keep the top K,
with K fixed in advance.

32
00:01:50,782 --> 00:01:52,212
This code uses 10.

33
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:55,436
One difference:
the lecture ranks by similarity,

34
00:01:55,436 --> 00:01:56,866
where higher is better.

35
00:01:57,186 --> 00:01:59,285
Chroma reports cosine distance,

36
00:01:59,285 --> 00:02:02,669
one minus the similarity,
where smaller is closer.

37
00:02:02,989 --> 00:02:05,122
Same order, opposite direction.

38
00:02:05,366 --> 00:02:10,891
And a query returns K results whenever
K chunks are available, relevant or not.

39
00:02:11,211 --> 00:02:13,964
Nothing in this code
sets a distance threshold.

40
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,323
In the code,
the query function sets the question,

41
00:02:17,323 --> 00:02:20,445
turns it into a vector
with the same Google model,

42
00:02:20,445 --> 00:02:22,818
and opens the chunk type's collection.

43
00:02:23,066 --> 00:02:28,378
Step 1: python cli.py --query
--chunk_type char-split.

44
00:02:28,698 --> 00:02:33,626
A small slip first: it prints load(),
a label copied from the load code.

45
00:02:33,946 --> 00:02:35,526
Then it connects to Chroma,

46
00:02:35,526 --> 00:02:38,494
but stops at the key
while embedding the question,

47
00:02:38,494 --> 00:02:39,621
before it searches.

48
00:02:39,866 --> 00:02:44,282
The question must become a vector
before the database can compare it,

49
00:02:44,282 --> 00:02:48,058
and only the same Google model
makes one in the same space.

50
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:50,732
Step 2 runs it on recursive-split.

51
00:02:51,052 --> 00:02:54,585
With your key,
look for 256 embedding values,

52
00:02:54,585 --> 00:02:56,705
the query, and the results.

53
00:02:57,025 --> 00:03:00,622
The results are lists of lists:
one list per query,

54
00:03:00,622 --> 00:03:03,937
holding IDs,
distances, metadata and documents.

55
00:03:04,257 --> 00:03:05,558
Pause and run both.

56
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:11,256
That's why chat, later, reads documents[0].

57
00:03:11,576 --> 00:03:15,992
No results were recorded,
so next we ask the loaded database directly.

58
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:20,981
Here's the query the code sends:
10 results and two filters.

59
00:03:21,301 --> 00:03:25,184
The metadata filter keeps one book,
The Complete Book of Cheese.

60
00:03:25,504 --> 00:03:28,789
The document filter keeps chunks
that contain Italian.

61
00:03:29,033 --> 00:03:30,996
The question is about Tolminc.

62
00:03:31,316 --> 00:03:33,236
What will the 10 results be about?

63
00:03:42,083 --> 00:03:45,176
An extra check counts them
in the loaded collection.

64
00:03:45,496 --> 00:03:51,854
Of 8,248 chunks,
the book filter keeps 1,355.

65
00:03:52,174 --> 00:03:54,200
The word Italian keeps 64.

66
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:57,144
How many mention Tolminc? None.

67
00:03:57,383 --> 00:03:58,471
Now a search.

68
00:03:58,791 --> 00:04:02,021
The real question needs Google
to become a vector,

69
00:04:02,021 --> 00:04:05,831
so we use a stand-in:
the stored vector of one story chunk.

70
00:04:06,151 --> 00:04:10,644
Without filters,
its 10 nearest are all from the story,

71
00:04:10,644 --> 00:04:14,727
at distances from 0,
the chunk itself, up to 0.16.

72
00:04:14,966 --> 00:04:19,489
With the code's filters, all 10 results come
from The Complete Book of Cheese.

73
00:04:19,809 --> 00:04:24,310
Their distances run from 0.28 to 0.31.

74
00:04:24,630 --> 00:04:26,156
The filters act first,

75
00:04:26,156 --> 00:04:30,732
and top K fills its 10 places
with whatever survives, however far.

76
00:04:30,966 --> 00:04:34,215
The lecture puts metadata
filtering after ranking:

77
00:04:34,215 --> 00:04:38,177
here, that would drop all 10 story
chunks and return nothing.

78
00:04:38,497 --> 00:04:42,422
Chroma filters first, so you get 10 results,
about the wrong cheese.

79
00:04:42,666 --> 00:04:46,554
A small slip:
the README promises various filters,

80
00:04:46,554 --> 00:04:48,421
but only variant 4 runs;

81
00:04:48,421 --> 00:04:51,029
variants 1 to 3 are commented out.

82
00:04:51,349 --> 00:04:55,274
Uncomment the first, with no filter,
and every chunk competes.

83
00:04:55,594 --> 00:04:59,072
And $contains is a keyword filter,
not a keyword ranking.

84
00:04:59,316 --> 00:05:03,690
The --get command from the restart
check uses the same book filter,

85
00:05:03,690 --> 00:05:05,844
but no question, so no distances.

86
00:05:06,164 --> 00:05:09,085
Its 10 records start
with the Gutenberg header,

87
00:05:09,085 --> 00:05:11,135
then the title page and contents.

88
00:05:11,455 --> 00:05:14,229
That header,
and each book's closing license,

89
00:05:14,229 --> 00:05:17,556
are indexed like any text:
strip them before chunking.

90
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:23,180
Now generation.

91
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:28,962
The lecture's final prompt has three parts:
a system prompt that says how to behave,

92
00:05:28,962 --> 00:05:32,993
the user's query,
and the context retrieved from the database.

93
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:38,119
In chat, the code queries again,
this time with no filter, for 10 results.

94
00:05:38,517 --> 00:05:41,504
Then it builds the input prompt:
the question,

95
00:05:41,504 --> 00:05:44,490
followed by the 10 chunks,
joined by newlines.

96
00:05:44,810 --> 00:05:47,946
The prompt goes to gemini-3.1-flash-lite.

97
00:05:48,183 --> 00:05:49,847
So where's the system prompt?

98
00:05:50,167 --> 00:05:53,337
Line 40 defines one:
answer only from the chunks,

99
00:05:53,337 --> 00:05:56,055
and say so when they don't contain enough.

100
00:05:56,375 --> 00:05:57,548
But look at the call.

101
00:05:57,868 --> 00:05:59,021
It's never sent.

102
00:05:59,267 --> 00:06:03,576
A small slip, with a one-line fix:
pass it in a config argument.

103
00:06:03,896 --> 00:06:06,094
The older pipenv branch did send it.

104
00:06:06,414 --> 00:06:09,713
Without it,
the README's promise of grounded answers

105
00:06:09,713 --> 00:06:11,363
rests on the chunks alone.

106
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:14,565
And notice what didn't change:
the model itself.

107
00:06:14,885 --> 00:06:16,165
No weights moved.

108
00:06:16,485 --> 00:06:19,237
Delete the collection,
and the knowledge is gone.

109
00:06:19,483 --> 00:06:26,310
Steps 1 and 2: python cli.py --chat,
for char-split and for recursive-split.

110
00:06:26,630 --> 00:06:29,446
Without a key,
both stop embedding the question.

111
00:06:29,766 --> 00:06:33,734
Each question costs one embedding call
and one Gemini call.

112
00:06:33,983 --> 00:06:38,942
With your key, look for the query,
printed with two question marks,

113
00:06:38,942 --> 00:06:42,346
a harmless slip,
and its 256 embedding values.

114
00:06:42,666 --> 00:06:46,535
Then the results, the number 10,
the input prompt with your chunks,

115
00:06:46,535 --> 00:06:47,978
and the model's response.

116
00:06:49,217 --> 00:06:50,924
Remember the opening question?

117
00:06:51,244 --> 00:06:54,026
The story is fiction,
so an answer about Mara,

118
00:06:54,026 --> 00:06:57,110
her grandfather
and the curd came from your chunks.

119
00:06:57,430 --> 00:07:01,078
Real-world rules for Tolminc came
from the model's memory.

120
00:07:01,398 --> 00:07:02,955
Compare both chunk types.

121
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:07,613
Step 3 is a link to the course's chat tool,
in Chrome.

122
00:07:07,933 --> 00:07:11,816
Pause: run both chat commands with your key,
then try the tool.

123
00:07:12,136 --> 00:07:17,405
For your own question,
change line 372 of cli.py.

124
00:07:20,750 --> 00:07:23,661
Back to chunking,
with the lecture's best answer:

125
00:07:23,661 --> 00:07:25,443
cut where the meaning changes.

126
00:07:25,763 --> 00:07:30,759
On the slides, an anchor walks
through the sentences and joins neighbors

127
00:07:30,759 --> 00:07:33,465
while their similarity stays above 0.8.

128
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:36,943
The code uses the same ingredients
with a different rule.

129
00:07:37,263 --> 00:07:39,145
Every sentence gets a window,

130
00:07:39,145 --> 00:07:43,428
itself and one neighbor on each side,
and each window is embedded.

131
00:07:43,667 --> 00:07:47,320
Then it measures the distance
between each window and the next,

132
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:50,045
and plots it over the text,
like a seismograph.

133
00:07:50,283 --> 00:07:53,397
Next, it draws a line
at the 95th percentile,

134
00:07:53,397 --> 00:07:55,680
and cuts at every spike above it.

135
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,371
So the biggest 5 percent of gaps
become chunk boundaries.

136
00:07:59,617 --> 00:08:02,156
char-split ran the chunk step without a key.

137
00:08:02,476 --> 00:08:03,862
Can semantic-split?

138
00:08:12,700 --> 00:08:18,417
No. To find where the meaning shifts,
it has to embed every sentence window first.

139
00:08:18,737 --> 00:08:21,606
Look at the function handed to the chunker:

140
00:08:21,606 --> 00:08:25,543
generate_text_embeddings,
the same one the embed step uses.

141
00:08:25,783 --> 00:08:30,391
The README's command runs all three steps
with --chunk_type semantic-split.

142
00:08:30,711 --> 00:08:33,574
On our machine it fails
inside the chunk step,

143
00:08:33,574 --> 00:08:36,685
on the first book,
before a single chunk is saved.

144
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:40,527
With your key, budget for it:
by our estimate,

145
00:08:40,527 --> 00:08:44,121
about 528 embedding calls
to chunk the corpus,

146
00:08:44,121 --> 00:08:48,496
about 90 more to embed the chunks,
and 10 to 20 minutes.

147
00:08:48,816 --> 00:08:52,357
Try one book first,
then put the others back and run it again.

148
00:08:52,677 --> 00:08:54,573
Look for a chunk count per book,

149
00:08:54,573 --> 00:08:58,544
and finally, finished inserting
into the semantic-split-collection.

150
00:08:58,783 --> 00:09:00,899
The chunks come out very uneven,

151
00:09:00,899 --> 00:09:04,735
and even a single-topic book
gets cut at its biggest gaps.

152
00:09:05,055 --> 00:09:08,042
Afterwards, an extra:
chat works the same way.

153
00:09:08,362 --> 00:09:10,175
Pause and try it, with your key.

154
00:09:13,517 --> 00:09:15,010
The lecture goes further.

155
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:19,533
Its frame adds a pre-retrieval stage
and a post-retrieval stage.

156
00:09:19,853 --> 00:09:22,461
Semantic chunking sits in pre-retrieval:

157
00:09:22,461 --> 00:09:25,592
the one advanced lever
this tutorial implements.

158
00:09:25,912 --> 00:09:28,344
Here are three more, as ideas only.

159
00:09:28,584 --> 00:09:31,379
Also before retrieval: query rewriting.

160
00:09:31,699 --> 00:09:34,902
A model turns a cluttered question
into a clean one,

161
00:09:34,902 --> 00:09:36,627
like where do dolphins live.

162
00:09:36,947 --> 00:09:40,616
But hand it a vague question,
and the rewrite can invent details.

163
00:09:41,900 --> 00:09:43,969
In retrieval: hybrid search.

164
00:09:44,289 --> 00:09:48,108
Keywords pin down names and rare words;
vectors catch meaning.

165
00:09:48,428 --> 00:09:52,353
Take the question: Chester went
to the bank of the river in the morning.

166
00:09:52,673 --> 00:09:55,020
The two rank three paragraphs differently.

167
00:09:55,267 --> 00:10:00,029
Reciprocal rank fusion gives
each paragraph one half divided by its rank,

168
00:10:00,029 --> 00:10:02,051
in each list, and adds them up.

169
00:10:02,371 --> 00:10:09,539
The near paraphrase wins with 0.75,
ahead of 0.67 and 0.42.

170
00:10:11,034 --> 00:10:13,125
After retrieval: re-ranking.

171
00:10:13,445 --> 00:10:14,563
A fast retriever,

172
00:10:14,563 --> 00:10:17,985
whose vectors were made
before the question existed,

173
00:10:17,985 --> 00:10:21,359
returns 25 candidates
with the right ones scattered.

174
00:10:21,679 --> 00:10:25,988
A slower model reads each question and chunk
together and reorders them.

175
00:10:27,284 --> 00:10:29,673
None of these is in cli.py.

176
00:10:29,993 --> 00:10:34,804
They're what you reach for when naive
retrieval brings back the wrong chunks;

177
00:10:34,804 --> 00:10:37,929
our filtered query
only needed its filter removed.

178
00:10:40,767 --> 00:10:42,111
Time to tear down.

179
00:10:42,431 --> 00:10:46,168
Type exit, then docker compose down
from the repo folder:

180
00:10:46,168 --> 00:10:50,431
the same harmless warnings,
then Chroma and the network are gone.

181
00:10:52,350 --> 00:10:56,799
The images stay:
2.11 gigabytes for the CLI image,

182
00:10:56,799 --> 00:11:01,694
its Python base included,
and 826 megabytes for Chroma.

183
00:11:01,934 --> 00:11:06,201
Now git status shows only your edit
to docker-shell.sh.

184
00:11:06,521 --> 00:11:09,641
The outputs/ and docker-volumes/
folders are ignored,

185
00:11:09,641 --> 00:11:10,936
and stay on your disk.

186
00:11:11,184 --> 00:11:15,429
Nothing keeps running in Google Cloud;
you paid only for what you sent.

187
00:11:15,749 --> 00:11:17,579
Keep the key beside the repo:

188
00:11:17,579 --> 00:11:21,872
inside it, git wouldn't ignore the key,
and the image would copy it.

189
00:11:22,192 --> 00:11:23,792
Delete it when the course ends.

190
00:11:24,112 --> 00:11:28,122
Pause, then tear down,
so port 8000 is free next time.

191
00:11:30,367 --> 00:11:31,420
One last lesson,

192
00:11:31,420 --> 00:11:34,975
from the staff's recorded run
of next lecture's agent.

193
00:11:35,295 --> 00:11:39,265
It retrieved text about transhumance
from an older Tolminc book,

194
00:11:39,265 --> 00:11:40,692
replaced that same day.

195
00:11:41,012 --> 00:11:46,068
The database answers from what was loaded:
when the books change, rebuild the index.

196
00:11:46,317 --> 00:11:49,197
Next lecture runs this command
with an agent.

197
00:11:49,517 --> 00:11:53,336
In the staff's run, the model chose
which retrieval tool to call.

198
00:11:53,656 --> 00:11:55,860
The lecture's Self-RAG goes further:

199
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:58,654
the model decides
whether to retrieve at all,

200
00:11:58,654 --> 00:12:00,269
then checks what it found.

201
00:12:00,589 --> 00:12:04,194
Our chat opens the book for every question,
and checks nothing.

202
00:12:05,434 --> 00:12:06,842
And the four questions?

203
00:12:07,162 --> 00:12:09,594
An embedding is a direction for meaning.

204
00:12:09,914 --> 00:12:13,626
A vector database stores records,
with an index to search them.

205
00:12:13,946 --> 00:12:16,698
A query finds
the question's nearest neighbors.

206
00:12:17,018 --> 00:12:20,196
The context is the chunks
you paste into the prompt.

207
00:12:20,434 --> 00:12:22,439
Five questions to test yourself.

208
00:12:22,759 --> 00:12:26,941
One: which flags belong to each lane,
and what must both lanes share?

209
00:12:27,261 --> 00:12:31,195
Two:
why does the CLI reach the database by name,

210
00:12:31,195 --> 00:12:33,042
and who uses port 8000?

211
00:12:33,284 --> 00:12:35,609
Three: what does overlap buy and cost?

212
00:12:35,929 --> 00:12:38,895
And why did the recursive splitter
make more chunks?

213
00:12:39,215 --> 00:12:42,863
Four: why did the README's query
return no Tolminc text?

214
00:12:43,183 --> 00:12:46,297
Five: what is the prompt missing,
and what's the fix?

215
00:12:46,534 --> 00:12:48,848
The answers are in the exercises,

216
00:12:48,848 --> 00:12:53,617
and every command is in the study guide,
in the MLOps Learning repo.

217
00:12:53,937 --> 00:12:55,558
Next time: agents.

